WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.009
John Carpenter's 1988 opus They Live is a
scathing indictment of capitalism and the

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:09.740
culture of greed masquerading as a B movie
about invading aliens.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.759
It stars professional wrestler "Rowdy" Roddy
Piper as a homeless laborer whose world is

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:19.210
turned upside down when he finds a box of
special sunglasses that allow the wearer to

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.340
see the truth about subliminal messaging forcing
humanity to OBEY.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.100
It’s one of Carpenter's greatest films,
but grab a pair of Hoffman lenses for yourself

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.900
to see the truth that even hardcore fans may
not know about They Live.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:32.600
H.P.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.000
Lovecraft, Ray Nelson, and Richard Armitage

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.620
Sharp-eyed viewers may have noticed that the
screenplay is credited to Frank Armitage,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.480
which happens to be the same name as Keith
David's character.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.260
This, of course, is a pseudonym.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.220
Carpenter wrote the screenplay but felt it
was enough of a collaborative effort with

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:52.910
his future wife Sandy King, and Roddy Piper
as well, that he didn’t take sole credit.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:57.300
Instead, he lifted the name “Armitage”
from The Dunwich Horror, a classic story by

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:57.800
H.P.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.740
Lovecraft, whose work often involved characters
being driven mad by discovering hidden truths.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.540
Lovecraft wasn’t the direct inspiration
for They Live, though.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.840
Instead, Carpenter drew from another short
story: Ray Nelson's "Eight O'Clock in the

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:10.340
Morning."

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:16.280
Published in 1963, and turned into a comic
book in 1986, it features a character named

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:20.960
Nada who awakens to the fact that the world
is secretly being run by snake-like aliens

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.460
who control humanity's thoughts and actions.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:27.880
While many of the story's elements would later
make it to the screen, Carpenter wisely added

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.720
a few tweaks to the plot and changed a few
elements to make it more believable.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.000
Or at least as believable as a story about
magic sunglasses that let you see aliens can

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:36.500
be.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.200
“I’m givin’ you a choice, either put
on these glasses, or start eating that trash

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:42.700
can.”

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.760
The Man With No Name

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.980
Like the lead character of Nelson’s short
story, Roddy Piper’s character is called

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.060
“Nada” because he is meant to be a blank
slate everyman.

00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:56.969
He is given very little backstory and is never
referred to by his name in the film even once,

00:01:56.969 --> 00:01:59.200
with “Nada” only appearing in the credits.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.130
In the years since, though, Carpenter has
let it slip that the character's first name

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:08.250
is John, and that he indeed has an extensive
backstory, even if the filmmaker himself doesn't

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.080
know what it is.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.060
“I’ve had a rough couple of days.”

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.890
In an interview, Carpenter revealed that only
one man knew Nada’s full origin story: Roddy

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:16.440
Piper.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.220
Before filming, Carpenter asked Piper to come
up with a backstory on his own, and further

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.120
told him to never share what it was with the
director, to lend his character more authenticity.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.060
True to his word, Piper never went on the
record before his untimely death in 2015,

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:35.870
meaning that Nada will always be what Carpenter
intended: a mystery to everyone, even the

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.940
man who wrote and directed the movie.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.660
The most brutal brawl in cinema history

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.300
At nearly six minutes long, the iconic alleyway
fight between Nada and Frank is one of the

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.670
most celebrated brawls in film history.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:52.079
It's been endlessly referenced, quoted and
parodied, and it’s inevitably brought up

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.900
as a point of comparison every time new contender
for the "best fight scene of all time" emerges.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.780
“...Oh man, I’m sorry!”

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:04.520
With the idea of creating a fight scene that
would be completely unique in films, Carpenter

00:03:04.521 --> 00:03:09.739
turned to stunt coordinator Jeff Imada, whose
work can be seen in action movies like Lethal

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:14.909
Weapon, The Bourne Conspiracy, and Furious
7, and to Piper, drawing on his experience

00:03:14.909 --> 00:03:16.350
as a professional wrestler.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.939
The result speaks for itself, but David complimented
Piper on helping to make the film more realistic,

00:03:20.939 --> 00:03:25.170
while Piper’s assessment of David makes
it clear that he’s no slouch either.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.040
"This guy hits like a mule!”

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.980
He's all out of bubblegum

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.709
Soon after Nada's unpleasant discovery about
the world around him, he has a scuffle with

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:38.760
a pair of cops and manages to get away with
one of their shotguns by ducking into a building,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.000
which turns out to be a bank.

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:44.340
As the patrons react to Nada with his shades
and shotgun, he utters one of the all-time

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.629
classic badass lines in cinema history:

00:03:46.629 --> 00:03:56.520
“I have come here to chew bubblegum and
kick ass … and I'm all out of bubblegum.”

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.040
The line has been embedded in pop culture
ever since, but it wasn't Carpenter's creation,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.960
it came directly from Piper.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:06.780
According to Carpenter, Piper carried a book
full of one-liners that he’d use in his

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.860
wrestling promos, and while he was writing
the script, Carpenter lifted the best one

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:11.460
for the scene.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.520
It’s just a shame he never got to use that
one on Hulk Hogan.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.400
Sticking it to Reagan

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:21.480
Carpenter's choice to make his heroes down-on-their-luck
homeless men up against alien invaders who

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.730
all seem to be rich yuppies (and, more frighteningly,
politicians) underlines the film's ultimate

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.520
message, which should be even clearer today
than it was 30 years ago.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.520
Carpenter has gone as far as calling it a
documentary about the exploitation of the

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:39.130
poor by the upper class and an indictment
of the rampant economic inequality that makes

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.390
it possible, and even filmed the opening in
an actual homeless settlement, paying the

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:44.700
residents as extras.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:50.360
If Carpenter intended They Live to be a ballistic
missile lobbed at '80s materialism and excess,

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.320
he's never been shy about who he saw as the
ultimate symbol of that excess: President

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.160
Ronald Reagan.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:59.300
Speaking with Starlog around the time of the
film's release, Carpenter was clear on this

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.500
point, calling the president and his cabinet
"a bunch of crooks.”

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.890
In the years since, Carpenter has been happy
to elaborate on his contempt for Reagan whenever

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:08.290
given the opportunity.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:12.810
In an interview with the Los Angeles Times
in 2013, Carpenter said that he’d “decided

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:17.100
I had to make a statement … and that's They
Live,” citing the literal and figurative

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.320
way that it “was giving the finger to Reagan
when nobody else would."

